/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Exodus chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("exodus", [
{n:16, name:"Bread from Heaven", seal:{ref:"Exodus 16:35",verse:"And the children of Israel did eat manna forty years, until they came to a land inhabited; they did eat manna, until they came unto the borders of the land of Canaan."}, facts:["Did you know the name manna comes from the Israelites' own question? Seeing the bread, they asked 'What is it?', and the name stuck.","Did you know the Sabbath was given here in the wilderness, before the Ten Commandments were spoken at Sinai? God taught Israel to rest while He fed them.","Did you know Israel ate manna for forty years, until they came to the borders of the land of Canaan?"], questions:[
{q:"From Elim, where did the congregation of Israel journey next?", options:["The wilderness of Sin","The wilderness of Paran","The wilderness of Zin","The wilderness of Shur"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:1",verse:"And they took their journey from Elim, and all the congregation of the children of Israel came unto the wilderness of Sin, which is between Elim and Sinai, on the fifteenth day of the second month after their departing out of the land of Egypt.", insight:"Every grumble in Israel's story starts somewhere real. God met His people in the wilderness of Sin not with a rebuke first, but with bread."},
{q:"What did the whole congregation do against Moses and Aaron in the wilderness of Sin?", options:["They murmured against them","They sang songs of praise to them","They offered them sacrifices","They asked them to be kings"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:2",verse:"And the whole congregation of the children of Israel murmured against Moses and Aaron in the wilderness:", insight:"Hunger has a way of rewriting memory. One complaint about bread made them forget the sea that had parted for them."},
{q:"What did the people say they wished had happened back in Egypt?", options:["That they had died by the hand of the LORD in Egypt with bread to eat","That Pharaoh had made them officers","That Egypt had taller walls","That they had never crossed the sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:3",verse:"And the children of Israel said unto them, Would to God we had died by the hand of the LORD in the land of Egypt, when we sat by the flesh pots, and when we did eat bread to the full; for ye have brought us forth into this wilderness, to kill this whole assembly with hunger.", insight:"They called slavery a feast because it was familiar. A fed chain can look kinder than a free wilderness, but it is still a chain."},
{q:"What did the LORD promise to rain from heaven for Israel?", options:["Bread","Silver","Wine","Quails at noon"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:4",verse:"Then said the LORD unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you; and the people shall go out and gather a certain rate every day, that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no.", insight:"God answered grumbling with generosity. Before He corrected their hearts, He filled their hands, a pattern of grace that keeps repeating."},
{q:"Why did the LORD say He would rain the bread from heaven?", options:["To prove whether they will walk in His law, or no","To see whether they could count it all","To make them stronger than the Egyptians","To teach them to love the desert"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:4",verse:"Then said the LORD unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you; and the people shall go out and gather a certain rate every day, that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no.", insight:"The manna was a daily exam, not in arithmetic but in trust. Each morning asked the same question: will you gather and obey today?"},
{q:"What covered the camp at even, as a sign of the LORD's promise?", options:["The quails","A cloud of locusts","A flock of doves","A swarm of bees"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:13",verse:"And it came to pass, that at even the quails came up, and covered the camp: and in the morning the dew lay round about the host.", insight:"Evening brought flesh and morning brought bread, just as He said. God keeps His word down to the hour."},
{q:"When the morning dew lifted, what lay upon the face of the wilderness?", options:["A small round thing, as small as the hoar frost","A scattering of silver coins","A field of white flowers","A layer of fine sand"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:14",verse:"And when the dew that lay was gone up, behold, upon the face of the wilderness there lay a small round thing, as small as the hoar frost on the ground.", insight:"God's provision rarely arrives looking grand. It came as frost on the ground, small enough to be overlooked, precious enough to live on."},
{q:"What did the children of Israel say to one another when they first saw the manna?", options:["It is manna, for they wist not what it was","It is honey, let us store it up","It is frost, let it pass away","It is dew, let it dry"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:15",verse:"And when the children of Israel saw it, they said one to another, It is manna: for they wist not what it was. And Moses said unto them, This is the bread which the LORD hath given you to eat.", insight:"They named the miracle after their own wonder. Sometimes the best theology starts as an honest question: what is this?"},
{q:"What did Moses tell the people the manna was?", options:["The bread which the LORD hath given you to eat","A trick of the wilderness wind","A gift sent by the Egyptians","A kind of sweet fungus"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:15",verse:"And when the children of Israel saw it, they said one to another, It is manna: for they wist not what it was. And Moses said unto them, This is the bread which the LORD hath given you to eat.", insight:"Moses gave them the only explanation that mattered. This was not nature's accident. This was the LORD's table, set in the desert."},
{q:"What happened to the manna that some left until the morning?", options:["It bred worms and stank","It turned into pure gold","It became sweeter than honey","It hardened into stone"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:20",verse:"Notwithstanding they hearkened not unto Moses; but some of them left of it until the morning, and it bred worms, and stank: and Moses was wroth with them.", insight:"Hoarding God's gift spoiled it. Grace was given fresh each morning, and yesterday's trust could not be saved up like treasure."},
{q:"What happened to the manna when the sun grew hot?", options:["It melted","It caught fire","It was carried off by birds","It sank into the ground"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:21",verse:"And they gathered it every morning, every man according to his eating: and when the sun waxed hot, it melted.", insight:"The manna would not wait for the lazy. God's provision rewards the one who rises early and gathers while it is given."},
{q:"How much manna did the people gather on the sixth day?", options:["Twice as much bread, two omers for one man","Half as much, for the LORD gave less","The same as every other day","None at all, for the sixth day was a fast"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:22",verse:"And it came to pass, that on the sixth day they gathered twice as much bread, two omers for one man: and all the rulers of the congregation came and told Moses.", insight:"The double portion was God's own planning for the rest to come. Provision and Sabbath were woven together from the very start."},
{q:"What did Moses call the seventh day, the morrow after the sixth?", options:["The rest of the holy sabbath unto the LORD","A day for marching on","A day for trading goods","A day for building tents"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:23",verse:"And he said unto them, This is that which the LORD hath said, To morrow is the rest of the holy sabbath unto the LORD: bake that which ye will bake to day, and seethe that ye will seethe; and that which remaineth over lay up for you to be kept until the morning.", insight:"Rest was commanded before the commandments were carved. God wanted His people to learn that He could be trusted with an empty day."},
{q:"What did some of the people do on the seventh day?", options:["They went out to gather and found none","They gathered double and hid it","They marched back toward Egypt","They refused to leave their tents"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:27",verse:"And it came to pass, that there went out some of the people on the seventh day for to gather, and they found none.", insight:"They searched for bread where God had said there would be none. Obedience often means staying in when everyone else goes out."},
{q:"How much manna was each man to gather for his household each day?", options:["An omer for every man","A bushel for every man","A basket for every man","As much as his cart could carry"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:16",verse:"This is the thing which the LORD hath commanded, Gather of it every man according to his eating, an omer for every man, according to the number of your persons; take ye every man for them which are in his tents.", insight:"God measured the portion Himself: an omer a head, no more, no less. His arithmetic always fits the need exactly."},
{q:"What did the manna taste like?", options:["Wafers made with honey","Sour grapes","Bitter herbs","Burnt barley"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:31",verse:"And the house of Israel called the name thereof Manna: and it was like coriander seed, white; and the taste of it was like wafers made with honey.", insight:"The bread of heaven tasted sweet, like honey on a wafer. God's discipline in the desert never canceled His sweetness."},
{q:"What happened when some gathered much and some gathered little?", options:["He that gathered much had nothing over, and he that gathered little had no lack","The strong seized from the weak","Much was wasted and little was hoarded","Moses fined the greedy ones"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:18",verse:"And when they did mete it with an omer, he that gathered much had nothing over, and he that gathered little had no lack; they gathered every man according to his eating.", insight:"God leveled the field Himself. In His economy, the one who gathered much had no surplus and the one who gathered little had no lack."},
{q:"What did Moses command Aaron to do with an omer of manna?", options:["Put it in a pot and lay it up before the LORD","Bury it deep in the wilderness","Feed it to the cattle","Send it back to Egypt as proof"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:33",verse:"And Moses said unto Aaron, Take a pot, and put an omer full of manna therein, and lay it up before the LORD, to be kept for your generations.", insight:"A pot of manna was kept as a memorial for generations unborn. Remembering God's provision is itself an act of worship."},
{q:"How many years did the children of Israel eat manna?", options:["Forty years","Four years","Fourteen years","Seventy years"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:35",verse:"And the children of Israel did eat manna forty years, until they came to a land inhabited; they did eat manna, until they came unto the borders of the land of Canaan.", insight:"For forty years the bread never failed. A whole generation grew up on daily grace, gathered one morning at a time."},
{q:"What is an omer?", options:["The tenth part of an ephah","The hundredth part of an ephah","A measure of wine","A weight of silver"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 16:36",verse:"Now an omer is the tenth part of an ephah.", insight:"The measure sounds small, an omer, a tenth of an ephah. But in God's hands, the small portion fed a nation."}
]},
{n:17, name:"Water from the Rock", seal:{ref:"Exodus 17:15",verse:"And Moses built an altar, and called the name of it Jehovah-nissi:"}, facts:["Did you know the place was given two names? Massah means 'tempting' and Meribah means 'chiding', a double warning carved into the map.","Did you know Israel's first battle as a free people was against Amalek at Rephidim? Joshua led it while Moses' hands were lifted on the hill.","Did you know the altar's name, Jehovah-nissi, means 'The LORD my banner'? The victory belonged to the LORD before it belonged to Israel."], questions:[
{q:"Where did the children of Israel camp and find no water to drink?", options:["At Rephidim","At Elim","At Marah","At Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:1",verse:"And all the congregation of the children of Israel journeyed from the wilderness of Sin, after their journeys, according to the commandment of the LORD, and pitched in Rephidim: and there was no water for the people to drink.", insight:"Rephidim had no water, but it had a purpose. God led them there on purpose, to teach them that He could give drink in a dry place."},
{q:"What did the people do to Moses at Rephidim?", options:["They chided with him, saying, Give us water that we may drink","They bowed down before him","They offered him gifts of gold","They asked him to lead them back"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:2",verse:"Wherefore the people did chide with Moses, and said, Give us water that we may drink. And Moses said unto them, Why chide ye with me? wherefore do ye tempt the LORD?", insight:"Chiding is complaining with teeth. They did not ask for water; they accused Moses, as if he had emptied the wells himself."},
{q:"What question did Moses put back to the people?", options:["Wherefore do ye tempt the LORD?","Wherefore do ye love the desert?","Wherefore do ye fear Pharaoh?","Wherefore do ye praise the idols?"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:2",verse:"Wherefore the people did chide with Moses, and said, Give us water that we may drink. And Moses said unto them, Why chide ye with me? wherefore do ye tempt the LORD?", insight:"Moses saw past the man to the Maker. Their quarrel with him was really a quarrel with God, and he named it plainly."},
{q:"What did Moses fear the angry people were almost ready to do to him?", options:["Stone him","Crown him","Bind him","Mock him"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:4",verse:"And Moses cried unto the LORD, saying, What shall I do unto this people? they be almost ready to stone me.", insight:"The deliverer of Israel cried out, 'they be almost ready to stone me.' Leading God's people can be the loneliest work on earth."},
{q:"What did the LORD tell Moses to take in his hand?", options:["His rod, wherewith he smote the river","A pitcher from the camp","A branch from a tree","A stone from the brook"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:5",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Go on before the people, and take with thee of the elders of Israel; and thy rod, wherewith thou smotest the river, take in thine hand, and go.", insight:"The rod that had judged Egypt would now bless Israel. God uses the same instrument for judgment and for mercy."},
{q:"Where was Moses to smite the rock?", options:["In Horeb","In Egypt","In Canaan","In Midian"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:6",verse:"Behold, I will stand before thee there upon the rock in Horeb; and thou shalt smite the rock, and there shall come water out of it, that the people may drink. And Moses did so in the sight of the elders of Israel.", insight:"Horeb, the mount of God, became the fountain of God. The rock was struck once, and water flowed for a thirsty nation."},
{q:"Who did the LORD tell Moses to take with him to the rock?", options:["The elders of Israel","The captains of thousands","The sons of Aaron","The children only"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:6",verse:"Behold, I will stand before thee there upon the rock in Horeb; and thou shalt smite the rock, and there shall come water out of it, that the people may drink. And Moses did so in the sight of the elders of Israel.", insight:"The elders were to witness it with their own eyes. God's works were never meant to be rumors; He wanted witnesses."},
{q:"What two names did Moses call the place where the people strove?", options:["Massah and Meribah","Elim and Marah","Sinai and Horeb","Succoth and Etham"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:7",verse:"And he called the name of the place Massah, and Meribah, because of the chiding of the children of Israel, and because they tempted the LORD, saying, Is the LORD among us, or not?", insight:"Two names for one failure. God lets our sinful moments be remembered, not to shame us forever, but to warn those who come after."},
{q:"Why was the place called Massah?", options:["Because they tempted the LORD there","Because the rock was split there","Because Amalek was beaten there","Because the water was sweet there"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:7",verse:"And he called the name of the place Massah, and Meribah, because of the chiding of the children of Israel, and because they tempted the LORD, saying, Is the LORD among us, or not?", insight:"To tempt the LORD is to demand proof He has already given. They had manna in the morning and quails at night, and still they tested Him."},
{q:"What did the LORD say to write for a memorial in a book?", options:["That He would utterly put out the remembrance of Amalek from under heaven","That Israel should never leave Rephidim","That Moses should carve the law on the rock","That Aaron should keep the rod forever"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:14",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Write this for a memorial in a book, and rehearse it in the ears of Joshua: for I will utterly put out the remembrance of Amalek from under heaven.", insight:"God ordered the victory written down before the battle's dust had settled. What God does deserves ink, not just memory."},
{q:"Who came and fought with Israel in Rephidim?", options:["Amalek","The Philistines","The Moabites","The Canaanites"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:8",verse:"Then came Amalek, and fought with Israel in Rephidim.", insight:"Amalek struck the weary at the rear. The first enemy of free Israel attacked the stragglers, as the enemy still does."},
{q:"Whom did Moses choose to lead the men out against Amalek?", options:["Joshua","Aaron","Hur","Caleb"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:9",verse:"And Moses said unto Joshua, Choose us out men, and go out, fight with Amalek: to morrow I will stand on the top of the hill with the rod of God in mine hand.", insight:"This is Joshua's first appearance, chosen by Moses for the sword. The man who would lead Israel into Canaan learned war at Rephidim."},
{q:"Where did Moses stand during the battle with Amalek?", options:["On the top of the hill, with the rod of God in his hand","In the midst of the camp","At the door of the tabernacle","On the shore of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:9",verse:"And Moses said unto Joshua, Choose us out men, and go out, fight with Amalek: to morrow I will stand on the top of the hill with the rod of God in mine hand.", insight:"Moses went up while Joshua went out. The battle was fought with swords in the valley and with prayer on the hill, and both mattered."},
{q:"Who went up to the hilltop with Moses?", options:["Aaron and Hur","Nadab and Abihu","Eldad and Medad","Joshua and Caleb"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:10",verse:"So Joshua did as Moses had said to him, and fought with Amalek: and Moses, Aaron, and Hur went up to the top of the hill.", insight:"Aaron and Hur climbed with him, not to fight but to hold. The greatest leaders need friends who will climb the hill beside them."},
{q:"What happened when Moses held up his hand in the battle?", options:["Israel prevailed; when he let it down, Amalek prevailed","The sun stood still in the sky","Fire fell from heaven on Amalek","The ground swallowed the enemy"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:11",verse:"And it came to pass, when Moses held up his hand, that Israel prevailed: and when he let down his hand, Amalek prevailed.", insight:"The tide of battle rose and fell with Moses' hand. God tied the victory to prayer so plainly that no soldier could miss it."},
{q:"What did the LORD swear He would do concerning Amalek?", options:["Have war with Amalek from generation to generation","Forgive Amalek and make peace","Forget Amalek entirely","Send Amalek back to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:16",verse:"For he said, Because the LORD hath sworn that the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation.", insight:"God swore war with Amalek from generation to generation. Some evils God does not merely defeat; He commits to ending them entirely."},
{q:"What did Aaron and Hur do for Moses on the hilltop?", options:["They stayed up his hands, the one on the one side and the other on the other side","They fanned him with palm branches","They sang psalms over him","They anointed his head with oil"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:12",verse:"But Moses’ hands were heavy; and they took a stone, and put it under him, and he sat thereon; and Aaron and Hur stayed up his hands, the one on the one side, and the other on the other side; and his hands were steady until the going down of the sun.", insight:"One man on each side, holding up the hands of prayer. Some victories are won by those who never lift a sword."},
{q:"How long did Aaron and Hur keep Moses' hands steady?", options:["Until the going down of the sun","Until the moon rose","Until the third hour","Until the trumpet sounded"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:12",verse:"But Moses’ hands were heavy; and they took a stone, and put it under him, and he sat thereon; and Aaron and Hur stayed up his hands, the one on the one side, and the other on the other side; and his hands were steady until the going down of the sun.", insight:"They did not hold on for a moment but until sunset. Steadfastness is measured in hours, not in intentions."},
{q:"What was the outcome of the battle with Amalek?", options:["Joshua discomfited Amalek and his people with the edge of the sword","Amalek made peace with Israel","Both armies withdrew at dusk","The battle ended with no victor"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:13",verse:"And Joshua discomfited Amalek and his people with the edge of the sword.", insight:"The sword did its work because prayer did its work first. Joshua's victory in the valley was won on the hilltop."},
{q:"What did Moses name the altar he built after the victory?", options:["Jehovah-nissi, which means The LORD my banner","Jehovah-jireh, the LORD will provide","Jehovah-shalom, the LORD is peace","El-bethel, the God of Bethel"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 17:15",verse:"And Moses built an altar, and called the name of it Jehovah-nissi:", insight:"He named the altar for the LORD, not for Joshua. The banner over every true victory reads the same: the LORD did this."}
]},
{n:18, name:"Jethro's Counsel", seal:{ref:"Exodus 18:11",verse:"Now I know that the LORD is greater than all gods: for in the thing wherein they dealt proudly he was above them."}, facts:["Did you know Jethro, the priest of Midian, confessed that the LORD is greater than all gods? His praise is one of the first Gentile confessions in Scripture.","Did you know Jethro's plan of rulers over thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens shaped Israel's order all the way to the promised land?","Did you know Eliezer's name means 'God was my help'? Moses named him after the LORD delivered him from the sword of Pharaoh."], questions:[
{q:"Who came to Moses in the wilderness where he encamped at the mount of God?", options:["Jethro, the priest of Midian, Moses' father in law","Pharaoh, the king of Egypt","Balak, the king of Moab","Abimelech, the king of Gerar"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:1",verse:"When Jethro, the priest of Midian, Moses’ father in law, heard of all that God had done for Moses, and for Israel his people, and that the LORD had brought Israel out of Egypt;", insight:"Jethro came because he had heard what God had done. A good report of God's works travels farther than any messenger."},
{q:"Whom did Jethro bring with him to Moses?", options:["Moses' wife and her two sons","Moses' mother and sister","The elders of Midian","Gifts of gold and silver"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:5",verse:"And Jethro, Moses’ father in law, came with his sons and his wife unto Moses into the wilderness, where he encamped at the mount of God:", insight:"He brought the family Moses had sent back to safety. Reunion was Jethro's gift before counsel was his business."},
{q:"Which son of Moses was named Gershom?", options:["The one named for his father's words, I have been an alien in a strange land","The younger son, born in the wilderness","The son who became a priest","The son who stayed in Midian"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:3",verse:"And her two sons; of which the name of the one was Gershom; for he said, I have been an alien in a strange land:", insight:"Gershom means 'stranger there.' Moses named his son after his own exile, a reminder that God's people are pilgrims in this world."},
{q:"Why was Moses' other son named Eliezer?", options:["Because the God of his father was his help and delivered him from Pharaoh's sword","Because he was born on the Sabbath","Because he was the firstborn","Because Jethro chose the name"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:4",verse:"And the name of the other was Eliezer; for the God of my father, said he, was mine help, and delivered me from the sword of Pharaoh:", insight:"Eliezer means 'God my help.' Moses turned his own deliverance into his son's name, so the testimony would outlive him."},
{q:"What did Moses do when his father in law came to him?", options:["He went out to meet him, did obeisance, and kissed him","He sent Aaron to receive him","He kept him waiting at the gate","He asked him to wait until the morrow"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:7",verse:"And Moses went out to meet his father in law, and did obeisance, and kissed him; and they asked each other of their welfare; and they came into the tent.", insight:"The lawgiver of Israel bowed to his father-in-law. Greatness that cannot show honor at home is not greatness at all."},
{q:"What did Moses tell Jethro when they asked each other of their welfare?", options:["All that the LORD had done unto Pharaoh and the Egyptians for Israel's sake","The names of all the tribes of Israel","The route they would take to Canaan","The number of the fighting men"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:8",verse:"And Moses told his father in law all that the LORD had done unto Pharaoh and to the Egyptians for Israel’s sake, and all the travail that had come upon them by the way, and how the LORD delivered them.", insight:"Moses rehearsed the whole story: the sea, the manna, the water. Telling what God has done is the oldest form of preaching."},
{q:"How did Jethro respond to Moses' account of the LORD's deliverance?", options:["He rejoiced for all the goodness which the LORD had done to Israel","He wept for the Egyptians who drowned","He doubted the story was true","He grew jealous of Moses' fame"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:9",verse:"And Jethro rejoiced for all the goodness which the LORD had done to Israel, whom he had delivered out of the hand of the Egyptians.", insight:"Jethro rejoiced, not envied. A heart that can celebrate another's blessing is a heart God can use."},
{q:"What did Jethro say when he blessed the LORD?", options:["Blessed be the LORD, who hath delivered you out of the hand of the Egyptians","Blessed be Pharaoh, who let you go","Blessed be the gods of Midian","Blessed be the rod of Moses"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:10",verse:"And Jethro said, Blessed be the LORD, who hath delivered you out of the hand of the Egyptians, and out of the hand of Pharaoh, who hath delivered the people from under the hand of the Egyptians.", insight:"The priest of Midian blessed the God of Israel. Deliverance turned an outsider into a worshiper."},
{q:"What did Jethro confess about the LORD?", options:["Now I know that the LORD is greater than all gods","The LORD is only the God of Israel","The gods of Egypt were stronger","The LORD is like the other gods"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:11",verse:"Now I know that the LORD is greater than all gods: for in the thing wherein they dealt proudly he was above them.", insight:"Seeing what God did to proud Egypt convinced him. The LORD needs no defense; His works are His witnesses."},
{q:"What offering did Jethro take for God?", options:["A burnt offering and sacrifices","A grain offering only","A drink offering only","No offering at all"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:12",verse:"And Jethro, Moses’ father in law, took a burnt offering and sacrifices for God: and Aaron came, and all the elders of Israel, to eat bread with Moses’ father in law before God.", insight:"Jethro's confession became worship. True belief always moves from words about God to an offering before God."},
{q:"How long did Moses sit to judge the people on the next day?", options:["From the morning unto the evening","For one hour at noon","Until the third hour","From evening to morning"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:13",verse:"And it came to pass on the morrow, that Moses sat to judge the people: and the people stood by Moses from the morning unto the evening.", insight:"Moses judged from sunrise to sunset while the people stood around him. He was doing good work in the worst possible way."},
{q:"What did Jethro ask when he saw Moses judging alone?", options:["What is this thing that thou doest to the people?","Where is the tabernacle thou buildest?","Who are these people before thee?","When will we march to Canaan?"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:14",verse:"And when Moses’ father in law saw all that he did to the people, he said, What is this thing that thou doest to the people? why sittest thou thyself alone, and all the people stand by thee from morning unto even?", insight:"A wise outsider saw in one day what Moses could not see in months. Sometimes we need another set of eyes on our work."},
{q:"What was Jethro's verdict on Moses judging the people alone?", options:["The thing that thou doest is not good","The thing that thou doest is perfect","The people are too rebellious to judge","The law is too hard for them"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:17",verse:"And Moses’ father in law said unto him, The thing that thou doest is not good.", insight:"'Not good' was the kindest hard truth Moses ever heard. The first thing ever called 'not good' in Scripture was man alone; the second was a leader alone."},
{q:"Why did Jethro say Moses' way of judging would fail?", options:["Thou wilt surely wear away, for the thing is too heavy for thee","The people would rise up against him","The Egyptians would hear of it","The law would be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:18",verse:"Thou wilt surely wear away, both thou, and this people that is with thee: for this thing is too heavy for thee; thou art not able to perform it thyself alone.", insight:"Even a Spirit-filled leader has limits. Jethro warned that good intentions cannot carry a weight God never meant one man to bear."},
{q:"What did Jethro tell Moses to teach the people?", options:["Ordinances and laws, and the way wherein they must walk","The songs of Egypt","The art of war","The names of the stars"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:20",verse:"And thou shalt teach them ordinances and laws, and shalt shew them the way wherein they must walk, and the work that they must do.", insight:"Jethro's first counsel was not about helpers but about teaching. A people who know the way need fewer judges."},
{q:"What kind of men was Moses to choose as rulers?", options:["Able men, such as fear God, men of truth, hating covetousness","Rich men with many flocks","Old men with long beards","Strong men skilled in war"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:21",verse:"Moreover thou shalt provide out of all the people able men, such as fear God, men of truth, hating covetousness; and place such over them, to be rulers of thousands, and rulers of hundreds, rulers of fifties, and rulers of tens:", insight:"Four marks for a leader: ability, reverence, truth, and freedom from greed. Three thousand years later, the standard has not changed."},
{q:"Over what groups were the chosen rulers to be set?", options:["Thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens","Tribes, families, and houses only","Cities, villages, and fields","Armies, chariots, and horsemen"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:21",verse:"Moreover thou shalt provide out of all the people able men, such as fear God, men of truth, hating covetousness; and place such over them, to be rulers of thousands, and rulers of hundreds, rulers of fifties, and rulers of tens:", insight:"From thousands down to tens, no group was too small for care. God's order reaches the ten as surely as it reaches the thousand."},
{q:"Which matters were the rulers to bring to Moses?", options:["Every great matter, but every small matter they should judge themselves","Only matters of war","Only matters of worship","None, for Moses would judge all"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:22",verse:"And let them judge the people at all seasons: and it shall be, that every great matter they shall bring unto thee, but every small matter they shall judge: so shall it be easier for thyself, and they shall bear the burden with thee.", insight:"Great matters to Moses, small matters to the rulers. Wisdom knows what to carry upstairs and what to settle downstairs."},
{q:"How did Moses respond to his father in law's counsel?", options:["He hearkened to the voice of his father in law, and did all that he had said","He rejected it as the advice of an outsider","He delayed deciding for forty days","He asked the people to vote on it"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:24",verse:"So Moses hearkened to the voice of his father in law, and did all that he had said.", insight:"Moses listened. The meekest man on earth proved it by taking correction from his father-in-law without a hint of pride."},
{q:"What did Jethro do after giving his counsel?", options:["He departed and went his way into his own land","He stayed and became a judge in Israel","He traveled with them to Canaan","He built an altar at Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 18:27",verse:"And Moses let his father in law depart; and he went his way into his own land.", insight:"Jethro left as quietly as he came, his work done. The best counselors do not stay to take the credit."}
]},
{n:19, name:"At Mount Sinai", seal:{ref:"Exodus 19:6",verse:"And ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests, and an holy nation. These are the words which thou shalt speak unto the children of Israel."}, facts:["Did you know Israel reached Sinai in the third month after leaving Egypt? From rescue at the sea to covenant at the mountain took about two months.","Did you know the people washed their clothes and set bounds around the mountain? Holiness had to be prepared for, not presumed.","Did you know Moses spoke and God answered him by a voice? The people heard thunder; Moses heard words."], questions:[
{q:"In which month after leaving Egypt did Israel come to the wilderness of Sinai?", options:["The third month","The first month","The seventh month","The twelfth month"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:1",verse:"In the third month, when the children of Israel were gone forth out of the land of Egypt, the same day came they into the wilderness of Sinai.", insight:"The third month, the same day they left Egypt's border season behind. God keeps appointments; the covenant came right on time."},
{q:"Where did Israel pitch their camp before the mount?", options:["In the wilderness of Sinai","In the wilderness of Paran","In the plains of Moab","By the shore of the Red Sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:2",verse:"For they were departed from Rephidim, and were come to the desert of Sinai, and had pitched in the wilderness; and there Israel camped before the mount.", insight:"They camped before the mount, the very mountain where Moses had seen the burning bush. The journey had come full circle."},
{q:"Where did Moses go when the LORD called to him?", options:["He went up unto God into the mount","He went down into the valley","He went into the tabernacle","He went to the shore of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:3",verse:"And Moses went up unto God, and the LORD called unto him out of the mountain, saying, Thus shalt thou say to the house of Jacob, and tell the children of Israel;", insight:"Moses went up while the people waited below. Covenant always begins with someone willing to climb toward God."},
{q:"What did the LORD remind Israel He had done to the Egyptians?", options:["He asked them to consider what He did unto the Egyptians and how He bare them","He asked them to forget the Egyptians entirely","He asked them to make peace with Egypt","He asked them to count the Egyptian dead"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:4",verse:"Ye have seen what I did unto the Egyptians, and how I bare you on eagles’ wings, and brought you unto myself.", insight:"Before one command was given, God rehearsed His grace. The covenant rests on what He has already done, not on what we promise to do."},
{q:"What picture did the LORD use for how He carried Israel?", options:["I bare you on eagles' wings, and brought you unto myself","I led you as a shepherd leads sheep","I carried you as a father carries a child","I bore you as the sea bears ships"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:4",verse:"Ye have seen what I did unto the Egyptians, and how I bare you on eagles’ wings, and brought you unto myself.", insight:"Eagles' wings: strong, swift, and high above danger. God did not drag Israel out of Egypt; He lifted them."},
{q:"What would Israel become if they obeyed His voice and kept His covenant?", options:["A peculiar treasure unto Him above all people","A kingdom greater than Egypt","A nation of merchants and traders","A people hidden from all nations"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:5",verse:"Now therefore, if ye will obey my voice indeed, and keep my covenant, then ye shall be a peculiar treasure unto me above all people: for all the earth is mine:", insight:"Peculiar treasure: God's own prized possession. Obedience was never about earning love; it was about living like the treasured."},
{q:"What two titles did the LORD give to Israel?", options:["A kingdom of priests, and an holy nation","A kingdom of warriors, and a mighty nation","A kingdom of farmers, and a wealthy nation","A kingdom of singers, and a joyful nation"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:6",verse:"And ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests, and an holy nation. These are the words which thou shalt speak unto the children of Israel.", insight:"A kingdom of priests meant every Israelite had a holy calling, not just the few. Holiness was the nation's job description."},
{q:"Whom did Moses call together to lay the LORD's words before them?", options:["The elders of the people","The captains of the host","The sons of Aaron","The heads of the tribes only"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:7",verse:"And Moses came and called for the elders of the people, and laid before their faces all these words which the LORD commanded him.", insight:"Moses laid it all before the elders first. God's word moves through leaders who hear it before the people do."},
{q:"How did all the people answer together?", options:["All that the LORD hath spoken we will do","We will do only what is easy","Let Moses decide for us","We need more time to consider"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:8",verse:"And all the people answered together, and said, All that the LORD hath spoken we will do. And Moses returned the words of the people unto the LORD.", insight:"Their answer was sincere and swift, and soon broken. Good intentions need more than enthusiasm; they need a changed heart."},
{q:"In what did the LORD say He would come to Moses?", options:["In a thick cloud","In a pillar of fire by night","In a whirlwind","In a still small voice"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:9",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Lo, I come unto thee in a thick cloud, that the people may hear when I speak with thee, and believe thee for ever. And Moses told the words of the people unto the LORD.", insight:"A thick cloud, so the people would hear God speak with Moses and believe him forever. God honored His servant publicly."},
{q:"What were the people to do to sanctify themselves for two days?", options:["Wash their clothes","Shave their heads","Fast from all food","Build new tents"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:10",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Go unto the people, and sanctify them to day and to morrow, and let them wash their clothes,", insight:"Wash their clothes: outward cleanness for an inward meeting. God asked them to prepare, because He was truly coming."},
{q:"Against which day were the people to be ready?", options:["The third day, when the LORD would come down upon mount Sinai","The seventh day of rest","The first day of the month","The day of the new moon"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:11",verse:"And be ready against the third day: for the third day the LORD will come down in the sight of all the people upon mount Sinai.", insight:"The third day. God gave them time to get ready, because meeting Him is never meant to be casual or rushed."},
{q:"What did Moses set about the mount?", options:["Bounds, that the people should not go up nor touch the border of it","A wall of stones","A hedge of thorns","A line of soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:12",verse:"And thou shalt set bounds unto the people round about, saying, Take heed to yourselves, that ye go not up into the mount, or touch the border of it: whosoever toucheth the mount shall be surely put to death:", insight:"Bounds around the mountain taught reverence by geography. Some lines are drawn not to punish but to protect."},
{q:"What would happen to whoever touched the mount?", options:["He shall be surely put to death","He shall be made a priest","He shall be sent back to Egypt","He shall pay a fine of silver"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:12",verse:"And thou shalt set bounds unto the people round about, saying, Take heed to yourselves, that ye go not up into the mount, or touch the border of it: whosoever toucheth the mount shall be surely put to death:", insight:"Death for touching the mountain sounds severe until you see the fire on it. God's holiness is not a suggestion; it is a consuming reality."},
{q:"How was the trespasser to be punished, since no hand should touch him?", options:["He shall surely be stoned, or shot through","He shall be driven out with whips","He shall be bound in chains","He shall be shaved and shamed"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:13",verse:"There shall not an hand touch it, but he shall surely be stoned, or shot through; whether it be beast or man, it shall not live: when the trumpet soundeth long, they shall come up to the mount.", insight:"No hand was to touch him, lest the holiness spread like fire. Even judgment was carried out at a reverent distance."},
{q:"What three signs came upon the mount on the third morning?", options:["Thunders and lightnings, and a thick cloud, with the voice of the trumpet","Rain and hail and snow","Earthquake and flood and fire","Darkness and silence and stillness"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:16",verse:"And it came to pass on the third day in the morning, that there were thunders and lightnings, and a thick cloud upon the mount, and the voice of the trumpet exceeding loud; so that all the people that was in the camp trembled.", insight:"Thunder, lightning, cloud, and trumpet: all of creation announced Him. When God comes down, nature itself testifies."},
{q:"What did the loud trumpet do to the people in the camp?", options:["They trembled","They sang for joy","They fell asleep","They began to dance"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:16",verse:"And it came to pass on the third day in the morning, that there were thunders and lightnings, and a thick cloud upon the mount, and the voice of the trumpet exceeding loud; so that all the people that was in the camp trembled.", insight:"They trembled, and rightly so. The fear of the LORD is not the enemy of worship; it is where worship begins."},
{q:"Where did Moses bring the people to meet with God?", options:["To the nether part of the mount","To the top of the mount","To the door of his tent","To the bank of a river"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:17",verse:"And Moses brought forth the people out of the camp to meet with God; and they stood at the nether part of the mount.", insight:"He brought them forth out of the camp to the foot of the mountain. God was not met in comfort but in consecration."},
{q:"What did mount Sinai look like when the LORD descended upon it in fire?", options:["The smoke ascended as the smoke of a furnace, and the whole mount quaked greatly","It shone like the sun at noon","It was covered in white snow","It bloomed with flowers"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:18",verse:"And mount Sinai was altogether on a smoke, because the LORD descended upon it in fire: and the smoke thereof ascended as the smoke of a furnace, and the whole mount quaked greatly.", insight:"Smoke like a furnace and a quaking mountain: this was no gentle visit. God came in glory that could be seen, heard, and felt."},
{q:"What happened when Moses spoke on the mountain?", options:["God answered him by a voice","The trumpet drowned him out","The people shouted him down","An angel spoke for him"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 19:19",verse:"And when the voice of the trumpet sounded long, and waxed louder and louder, Moses spake, and God answered him by a voice.", insight:"Moses spoke, and God answered by a voice. In all the thunder, God still conversed with His servant like a friend."}
]},
{n:20, name:"The Ten Commandments", seal:{ref:"Exodus 20:2",verse:"I am the LORD thy God, which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage."}, facts:["Did you know the commandments begin with what God already did? 'I have brought thee out of the land of Egypt' comes before the first 'thou shalt not.'","Did you know the fifth commandment is the only one with a promise attached? Honour carries 'that thy days may be long.'","Did you know the altar had to be of earth or unhewn stone? If a tool was lifted upon it, the stone was polluted."], questions:[
{q:"How does the LORD introduce Himself before giving the commandments?", options:["I am the LORD thy God, which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage","I am the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob only","I am the LORD who dwells in the heavens above","I am the God who never speaks to men"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:2",verse:"I am the LORD thy God, which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage.", insight:"Before one 'thou shalt,' God says 'I have brought thee out.' Obedience grows from remembered redemption, not from bare duty."},
{q:"What is the first commandment?", options:["Thou shalt have no other gods before me","Thou shalt make no journey on the Sabbath","Thou shalt offer a lamb every morning","Thou shalt build me a temple of gold"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:3",verse:"Thou shalt have no other gods before me.", insight:"No other gods before Me: first place or no place. God will not share the throne of the heart with rivals."},
{q:"What does the second commandment forbid making?", options:["Any graven image, or any likeness of any thing in heaven, earth, or water","Any altar of earth","Any song of praise","Any copy of the law"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:4",verse:"Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, or any likeness of any thing that is in heaven above, or that is in the earth beneath, or that is in the water under the earth:", insight:"No carved gods, because no carving can contain Him. An image of God always shrinks God, and shrunken gods make shrunken worshipers."},
{q:"What does the second commandment forbid doing before images?", options:["Bowing down to them nor serving them","Looking upon them at all","Selling them in the market","Carrying them in procession only"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:5",verse:"Thou shalt not bow down thyself to them, nor serve them: for I the LORD thy God am a jealous God, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation of them that hate me;", insight:"Not bow, not serve: worship belongs to the Maker, not the made. The heart was built to kneel, but only before its Creator."},
{q:"To whom does God show mercy, according to the second commandment?", options:["Unto thousands of them that love Him and keep His commandments","Unto the rich who give much","Unto the priests alone","Unto those who never sin"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:6",verse:"And shewing mercy unto thousands of them that love me, and keep my commandments.", insight:"Mercy to thousands who love Him. God's judgment is measured; His mercy overflows the measuring cup."},
{q:"What does the third commandment forbid?", options:["Taking the name of the LORD thy God in vain","Speaking the name of the LORD at all","Writing the name of the LORD","Singing the name of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:7",verse:"Thou shalt not take the name of the LORD thy God in vain; for the LORD will not hold him guiltless that taketh his name in vain.", insight:"His name is not a filler word or a curse. To take it in vain is to treat the Holy as hollow, and God will not hold that guiltless."},
{q:"What does the fourth commandment say to do with the Sabbath day?", options:["Remember it, to keep it holy","Forget it, for work is holier","Mourn on it, for sin is great","Travel on it, for the road is clear"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:8",verse:"Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy.", insight:"Remember the Sabbath: rest is commanded, not suggested. God built a pause into creation because humans forget to stop."},
{q:"How many days does the fourth commandment appoint for labour?", options:["Six days shalt thou labour, and do all thy work","Five days, with two for rest","Seven days, without ceasing","Three days, for God provides the rest"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:9",verse:"Six days shalt thou labour, and do all thy work:", insight:"Six days for work dignifies labour. The commandment honors the workweek before it hallows the rest."},
{q:"Who is to do no work on the seventh day?", options:["Thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, nor thy servant, nor thy cattle, nor thy stranger","Thou alone, for the rest are exempt","Only the priests and Levites","Only the men, not the women"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:10",verse:"But the seventh day is the sabbath of the LORD thy God: in it thou shalt not do any work, thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, thy manservant, nor thy maidservant, nor thy cattle, nor thy stranger that is within thy gates:", insight:"Rest reached the servant, the stranger, even the cattle. God's Sabbath is wide enough for the whole household, high and low."},
{q:"Why is the seventh day blessed and hallowed?", options:["For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, and rested the seventh day","For Moses commanded it at Sinai","For Israel needed a market day","For the Egyptians kept it too"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:11",verse:"For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day: wherefore the LORD blessed the sabbath day, and hallowed it.", insight:"The Sabbath is rooted in creation itself, older than Israel. When we rest, we imitate the Maker on His seventh day."},
{q:"What promise comes with honouring father and mother?", options:["That thy days may be long upon the land which the LORD thy God giveth thee","That thou shalt become a ruler","That thou shalt never know sorrow","That thy children shall be many"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:12",verse:"Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which the LORD thy God giveth thee.", insight:"Honour carries a promise: long days in the land. God ties the health of a nation to the honour given at home."},
{q:"What is the sixth commandment?", options:["Thou shalt not kill","Thou shalt not hate","Thou shalt not fight","Thou shalt not curse"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:13",verse:"Thou shalt not kill.", insight:"Two words in the Hebrew: no murder. Every human life bears God's image, and no one may erase it."},
{q:"What is the seventh commandment?", options:["Thou shalt not commit adultery","Thou shalt not divorce","Thou shalt not marry","Thou shalt not be alone"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:14",verse:"Thou shalt not commit adultery.", insight:"Marriage is a covenant, not a convenience. God guards it with a command because He designed it as a picture of His own faithfulness."},
{q:"What is the eighth commandment?", options:["Thou shalt not steal","Thou shalt not borrow","Thou shalt not trade","Thou shalt not work"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:15",verse:"Thou shalt not steal.", insight:"Four words that protect every worker's wage and every owner's goods. God calls honest gain holy and theft an assault on trust."},
{q:"What is the ninth commandment?", options:["Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour","Thou shalt not speak at all","Thou shalt not gossip in secret","Thou shalt not remain silent"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:16",verse:"Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour.", insight:"Truth in court, truth in conversation. A false word can hang an innocent man, so God guards the tongue with a command."},
{q:"What does the tenth commandment forbid coveting?", options:["Thy neighbour's house, wife, servants, ox, ass, and any thing that is thy neighbour's","Thy neighbour's wisdom","Thy neighbour's prayers","Thy neighbour's good name"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:17",verse:"Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour’s house, thou shalt not covet thy neighbour’s wife, nor his manservant, nor his maidservant, nor his ox, nor his ass, nor any thing that is thy neighbour’s.", insight:"Coveting is the sin no one sees, so God named it last. He judges the heart's wanting, not just the hand's taking."},
{q:"What did the people see and hear that made them stand afar off?", options:["The thunderings, and the lightnings, and the noise of the trumpet, and the mountain smoking","An army of angels with swords","A river of fire flowing down","A pillar of cloud by day"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:18",verse:"And all the people saw the thunderings, and the lightnings, and the noise of the trumpet, and the mountain smoking: and when the people saw it, they removed, and stood afar off.", insight:"They saw the thunder and stood afar off. Seeing God's glory up close made them feel the distance sin had put between them."},
{q:"What did the people beg Moses to do?", options:["Speak thou with us, and we will hear; but let not God speak with us, lest we die","Go up and bring the law down quickly","Build us gods to go before us","Lead us back to Egypt at once"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:19",verse:"And they said unto Moses, Speak thou with us, and we will hear: but let not God speak with us, lest we die.", insight:"They wanted Moses between them and the voice. A mediator is mercy: God gave them one then, and gave us Christ forever."},
{q:"Why did Moses say God had come to them?", options:["To prove them, and that His fear may be before their faces, that they sin not","To destroy them for their murmuring","To make them kings over the nations","To show them the land of Canaan"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:20",verse:"And Moses said unto the people, Fear not: for God is come to prove you, and that his fear may be before your faces, that ye sin not.", insight:"Fear not, Moses said, and then explained the fear. God's awesome presence was meant to keep them from sin, not to crush them."},
{q:"What kind of altar did the LORD command Israel to make?", options:["An altar of earth","An altar of gold","An altar of bronze","An altar of cedar wood"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 20:24",verse:"An altar of earth thou shalt make unto me, and shalt sacrifice thereon thy burnt offerings, and thy peace offerings, thy sheep, and thine oxen: in all places where I record my name I will come unto thee, and I will bless thee.", insight:"An altar of earth: the simplest materials for the holiest work. God is not impressed by our polish; He is pleased by our obedience."}
]}
]);
